            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Vertical Motion and Clouds ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 28.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              112.5199966430664,
              75.60729217529297,
              432.9850158691406,
              103.62728881835938
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "12.1",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              117.86051177978516,
              94.75901794433594,
              130.88050842285156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Introduction ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              103.5199966430664,
              117.86051177978516,
              171.03500366210938,
              130.88050842285156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A cloud is a visible aggregate of minute water droplets and/or ice particles in the atmosphere above the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              138.60244750976562,
              542.30615234375,
              149.5824432373047
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Earth’s surface. Fog differs from clouds only in that the base of fog is at the Earth’s surface while clouds ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              151.20242309570312,
              542.711669921875,
              162.1824188232422
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "are above the surface. Clouds are like signposts in the sky that provide information on air motion, stability, ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              163.86245727539062,
              542.6304321289062,
              174.8424530029297
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "and moisture. Clouds help pilots visualize weather conditions and potential weather hazards. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              176.52243041992188,
              482.0050048828125,
              187.50242614746094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Clouds form in the atmosphere as a result of condensation of water vapor in rising currents of air, or by the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              195.18246459960938,
              542.5256958007812,
              206.16246032714844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "evaporation of the lowest layer of fog. Rising currents of air are necessary for the formation of vertically ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              207.84243774414062,
              542.3610229492188,
              218.8224334716797
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "deep clouds capable of producing precipitation heavier than light intensity. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              220.50247192382812,
              404.125,
              231.4824676513672
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 146,
        "elements": []
      },
      {
        "page_number": 147,
        "elements": []
      },
      {
        "page_number": 148,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "while relative humidity increases until the parcel achieves saturation at its LCL of 2,000 ft. As the parcel ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              73.42244720458984,
              542.7280883789062,
              84.40245056152344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "continues to ascend, condensation produces cloud formation. Because the heat added during condensation ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              86.0824203491211,
              542.3719482421875,
              97.06242370605469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "offsets some of the cooling due to expansion, the parcel now cools at the moist adiabatic lapse rate of 2°C ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              98.68245697021484,
              542.8049926757812,
              109.66246032714844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "per 1,000 ft. The parcel’s dewpoint decreases at an identical rate as the lost water vapor condenses to form ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              111.3424301147461,
              542.5894165039062,
              122.32243347167969
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the cloud. The relative humidity of the ascending saturated (i.e., cloudy) parcel remains constant at about ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              124.02242279052734,
              542.7625122070312,
              135.00242614746094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "100 percent. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              136.68246459960938,
              129.125,
              147.66246032714844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A descending saturated air parcel quickly becomes unsaturated (see Figure 12-3). Its temperature increases ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              155.34243774414062,
              542.7244262695312,
              166.3224334716797
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "at 3°C per 1,000 ft, while its dewpoint increases at 0.5°C per 1,000 ft (see Table 12-1). The ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              168.00247192382812,
              542.7670288085938,
              178.9824676513672
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "temperature-dewpoint spread increases while relative humidity decreases. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              180.66244506835938,
              399.44500732421875,
              191.64244079589844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p148_b7.png",
            "bbox": [
              90.0,
              208.34002685546875,
              521.989990234375,
              531.6400146484375
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 12-3. Descending Air Parcel Example ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              544.1550903320312,
              255.0050048828125,
              554.1751098632812
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "At 5,000 ft, both the temperature and dewpoint of the air parcel are 6°C, indicating that it is saturated. As ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              568.702392578125,
              542.7050170898438,
              579.6824340820312
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the parcel descends, it quickly becomes unsaturated. Its temperature increases 3°C per 1,000 ft, while its ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              581.3624267578125,
              542.7485961914062,
              592.3424682617188
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "dewpoint increases at 0.5°C per 1,000 ft. The temperature-dewpoint spread increases while relative ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              594.0223999023438,
              542.6510009765625,
              605.00244140625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "humidity decreases until the parcel reaches the surface. Note that the parcel is now much warmer and drier ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              606.6224365234375,
              542.752197265625,
              617.6024780273438
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "at the surface than when it began the vertical motion process in Figure 12-2. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              619.2824096679688,
              409.5249938964844,
              630.262451171875
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
